# Use Arrow Functions

Always define functions as `const` arrow functions. Do not use `function` declarations.

```ts
// ❌ BAD
function getTotal(items: Item[]) {
  return items.reduce((sum, i) => sum + i.price, 0);
}
export function formatNaira(value: number) { /* ... */ }
export default function Page() { return <div />; }

// ✅ GOOD
const getTotal = (items: Item[]) => items.reduce((sum, i) => sum + i.price, 0);
export const formatNaira = (value: number) => { /* ... */ };
const Page = () => <div />;
export default Page;
```

## Exceptions (a `function` declaration is acceptable)

- TypeScript **function overloads** (multiple signatures + one implementation).
- Functions that rely on `this` binding or the `arguments` object.
- Generator functions (`function*`).

## Notes

- React components are arrow consts; for default exports, declare the const then `export default Name`.
- In `.tsx`, write generics with a trailing comma to avoid JSX ambiguity: `const f = <T,>(x: T) => x;`.
- Arrow functions are not hoisted — define a helper before the code that uses it at module-eval time.
